\\nrich                     13.7.1\\nrsa                      4.9\\ns3transfer               0.10.1\\nscreen-resolution-extra  0.0.0\\nSecretStorage            3.3.3\\nsetuptools               68.1.2\\nsix                      1.16.0\\nSQLAlchemy               1.4.50\\nssh-import-id            5.11\\nsystemd-python           235\\ntestresources            2.0.1\\nubuntu-drivers-common    0.0.0\\nubuntu-pro-client        8001\\nufw                      0.36.2\\nunattended-upgrades      0.1\\nunittest2                1.1.0\\nuritemplate              4.1.1\\nurllib3                  2.0.7\\nusb-creator              0.3.16\\nwadllib                  1.3.6\\nwebencodings             0.5.1\\nwheel                    0.42.0\\nxdg                      5\\nxkit                     0.0.0\\nyubikey-manager          5.2.1\\nzipp                     1.0.0\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "24812d1f-ae3b-41d9-8a11-00989acd408b", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 13}
{"id": "1a9f73a7-25d6-4546-9d7d-747b1aeccb64", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T23:20:11.371194", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "071c4b33-d089-421a-a34f-4934ae0e0dd2", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### Proposed Experimental Design Approach\n\n#### 1. Objective and Core Hypothesis\nThe study aims to evaluate pipeline parallelism (PP) schedules—namely standard **1F1B**, interleaved **1F1B-I** ($v=2$), Zero Bubble 1-partition (**ZB-1p**), and Zero Bubble 2-partition (**ZB-2p**, $v=2$)—to test the hypothesis:\n- **1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p** have structural pipeline bubble overheads that scale inversely with microbatch count ($\\sim \\mathcal{O}(p/m)$). Thus, their pipeline efficiency and system throughput improve significantly as the microbatch count $m$ increases.\n- **ZB-2p** partitions each device into 2 virtual stages and splits the backward pass into activation gradient computation ($B$) and parameter weight gradient computation ($W$). By scheduling $W$ into idle slots and interleaving the two partitions, ZB-2p eliminates virtually all warmup and cooldown bubbles even at small microbatch counts ($m \\sim p$ or $m \\sim 2p$), maintaining near-peak throughput without requiring a large $m$.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Experimental Setup and Design Matrix\n\n1. **System & Model Specifications:**\n   - **Model Architecture:** Standard decoder-only Transformer (e.g., LLaMA-7B or 13B configuration: sequence length 2048, hidden dimension 4096/5120, 32/40 layers).\n   - **Pipeline Depth ($p$):** $p = 4$ and $p = 8$ stages across GPU devices (e.g., 8 $\\times$ NVIDIA A100/H100 80GB SXM connected via NVLink).\n   - **Per-microbatch Batch Size ($b$):** Fixed at $b = 1$ or $b = 2$ sequences per microbatch to keep single-step computation times ($F$, $B$, $W$) strictly invariant across runs.\n\n2. **Independent Variables:**\n   - **PP Schedules:**\n     - `1F1B`: Conventional non-interleaved schedule (backward combined $B+W$).\n     - `1F1B-I`: Interleaved 1F1B with virtual stages $v = 2$.\n     - `ZB-1p`: Single partition per worker, backward split into $B$ and $W$.\n     - `ZB-2p`: Two partitions per worker ($v = 2$), backward split into $B$ and $W$ with zero-bubble scheduling.\n   - **Microbatch Count ($m$):** Swept across values relative to pipeline depth $p$:\n     - For $p = 8$: $m \\in \\{8, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64\\}$ (i.e., $m/p \\in \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8\\}$).\n\n3. **Dependent Variables & Metrics:**\n   - **Throughput:**\n     - Primary: End-to-end throughput in tokens/second ($\\text{tokens/s} = \\frac{m \\times b \\times \\text{seq\\_len}}{\\Delta t_{\\text{iteration}}}$).\n     - Secondary: Model TFLOPS per device.\n   - **Pipeline Bubble Rate:** Bubble fraction $r_{\\text{bubble}} = \\frac{T_{\\text{idle}}}{T_{\\text{total}}} = 1 - \\frac{m(t_F + t_B + t_W)}{p \\cdot T_{\\text{iteration}}}$.\n   - **Step Latency:** Iteration time (seconds) averaged over steady-state steps (excluding warmup iterations).\n   - **Peak Memory Consumption:** Max allocated GPU memory (GB) per device across the schedule.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Execution & Validation Plan\n- **Phase A (Schedule Simulation & Profiling):** Generate schedule execution DAGs using precise execution profiles of individual kernels ($F$, $B$, $W$) and P2P communication latencies to analytically verify bubble fractions across all $(m, \\text{schedule})$ combinations.\n- **Phase B (Empirical Pipeline Benchmarking):** Execute synthetic benchmark runs under identical distributed seeds and synthetic datasets for 10 warmup steps followed by 30 recorded steps per configuration.\n- **Phase C (Comparative Analysis & Curve Fitting):** Plot throughput and bubble fraction as functions of $m$ (or $m/p$). Fit saturation curves to verify:\n  - Steep throughput slope for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p at $m/p \\le 4$.\n  - Flat/near-saturated high throughput curve for ZB-2p starting at $m/p = 2$.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Consequential Decisions to Confirm\n- **Memory Management:** For large $m$, activation memory increases linearly unless activation checkpointing is applied. We plan to fix activation checkpointing policy identically across all methods to ensure fair latency comparison.\n- **Backward Splitting ($B$ vs $W$):** The backward pass must be decomposed into input gradient calculation ($B$) and weight gradient calculation ($W$), typically yielding execution time ratios $t_F : t_B : t_W \\approx 1 : 1 : 1$. We will record kernel timings to verify this balance.", "evidence": "- Environment inspection of /workspace revealed only `task.json`, with `track.md` stating: 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.'\n- The environment has no GPU enabled and no preinstalled third-party Zero Bubble or Megatron-LM codebase.\n- Theoretical foundation:\n  * In standard 1F1B, the bubble rate is (p - 1) / (m + p - 1), which directly decreases as m increases.\n  * In 1F1B-I with v=2, the bubble rate is (p - 1) / (v*m + p - 1), still dependent on 1/m.\n  * In ZB-1p, splitting backward into B and W fills the steady-state bubble, but warmup/cooldown bubbles of size ~(p - 1)*F remain, yielding bubble fraction ~ (p - 1) / (3m + p - 1), which also improves with increasing m.\n  * In ZB-2p, combining v=2 partitions with B/W splitting allows forward passes of partition 2 to overlap with partition 1 warmup and W to fill remaining bubbles, eliminating the bubble at m >= 2p and maintaining high throughput even at small m.", "assumptions": ["Computation time ratios follow standard Transformer scaling where forward (F), activation backward (B), and weight backward (W) approximate 1 : 1 : 1 (F ≈ B ≈ W).", "Per-microbatch batch size b and sequence length are held constant so that increasing m scales total global batch size without altering individual kernel latencies.", "GPU memory is sufficient (with standard selective activation checkpointing) to accommodate peak activation storage at the highest evaluated microbatch counts (m = 8p).", "P2P communication latency between pipeline stages is either non-blocking/overlapped or uniform across all stages."], "uncertainties": ["Impact of communication overhead: 1F1B-I and ZB-2p double the number of cross-stage communications (v=2), which could introduce non-negligible communication overhead if network bandwidth is constrained.", "Activation memory pressure: ZB-2p holds activations across two virtual stages, which may limit the maximum achievable microbatch count m without out-of-memory errors compared to 1F1B."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_428285__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_428285__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Propose experimental design to evaluate throughput scaling across PP schedules.\", \"answer\": \"### Proposed Experimental Design Approach\\n\\n#### 1. Objective and Core Hypothesis\\nThe study aims to evaluate pipeline parallelism (PP) schedules\\u2014namely standard **1F1B**, interleaved **1F1B-I** ($v=2$), Zero Bubble 1-partition (**ZB-1p**), and Zero Bubble 2-partition (**ZB-2p**, $v=2$)\\u2014to test the hypothesis:\\n- **1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p** have structural pipeline bubble overheads that scale inversely with microbatch count ($\\\\sim \\\\mathcal{O}(p/m)$). Thus, their pipeline efficiency and system throughput improve significantly as the microbatch count $m$ increases.\\n- **ZB-2p** partitions each device into 2 virtual stages and splits the backward pass into activation gradient computation ($B$) and parameter weight gradient computation ($W$). By scheduling $W$ into idle slots and interleaving the two partitions, ZB-2p eliminates virtually all warmup and cooldown bubbles even at small microbatch counts ($m \\\\sim p$ or $m \\\\sim 2p$), maintaining near-peak throughput without requiring a large $m$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 2. Experimental Setup and Design Matrix\\n\\n1. **System & Model Specifications:**\\n   - **Model Architecture:** Standard decoder-only Transformer (e.g., LLaMA-7B or 13B configuration: sequence length 2048, hidden dimension 4096/5120, 32/40 layers).\\n   - **Pipeline Depth ($p$):** $p = 4$ and $p = 8$ stages across GPU devices (e.g., 8 $\\\\times$ NVIDIA A100/H100 80GB SXM connected via NVLink).\\n   - **Per-microbatch Batch Size ($b$):** Fixed at $b = 1$ or $b = 2$ sequences per microbatch to keep single-step computation times ($F$, $B$, $W$) strictly invariant across runs.\\n\\n2. **Independent Variables:**\\n   - **PP Schedules:**\\n     - `1F1B`: Conventional non-interleaved schedule (backward combined $B+W$).\\n     - `1F1B-I`: Interleaved 1F1B with virtual stages $v = 2$.\\n     - `ZB-1p`: Single partition per worker, backward split into $B$ and $W$.\\n     - `ZB-2p`: Two partitions per worker ($v = 2$), backward split into $B$ and $W$ with zero-bubble scheduling.\\n   - **Microbatch Count ($m$):** Swept across values relative to pipeline depth $p$:\\n     - For $p = 8$: $m \\\\in \\\\{8, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64\\\\}$ (i.e., $m/p \\\\in \\\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8\\\\}$).\\n\\n3. **Dependent Variables & Metrics:**\\n   - **Throughput:**\\n     - Primary: End-to-end throughput in tokens/second ($\\\\text{tokens/s} = \\\\frac{m \\\\times b \\\\times \\\\text{seq\\\\_len}}{\\\\Delta t_{\\\\text{iteration}}}$).\\n     - Secondary: Model TFLOPS per device.\\n   - **Pipeline Bubble Rate:** Bubble fraction $r_{\\\\text{bubble}} = \\\\frac{T_{\\\\text{idle}}}{T_{\\\\text{total}}} = 1 - \\\\frac{m(t_F + t_B + t_W)}{p \\\\cdot T_{\\\\text{iteration}}}$.\\n   - **Step Latency:** Iteration time (seconds) averaged over steady-state steps (excluding warmup iterations).\\n   - **Peak Memory Consumption:** Max allocated GPU memory (GB) per device across the schedule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 3. Execution & Validation Plan\\n- **Phase A (Schedule Simulation & Profiling):** Generate schedule execution DAGs using precise execution profiles of individual kernels ($F$, $B$, $W$) and P2P communication latencies to analytically verify bubble fractions across all $(m, \\\\text{schedule})$ combinations.\\n- **Phase B (Empirical Pipeline Benchmarking):** Execute synthetic benchmark runs under identical distributed seeds and synthetic datasets for 10 warmup steps followed by 30 recorded steps per configuration.\\n- **Phase C (Comparative Analysis & Curve Fitting):** Plot throughput and bubble fraction as functions of $m$ (or $m/p$). Fit saturation curves to verify:\\n  - Steep throughput slope for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p at $m/p \\\\le 4$.\\n  - Flat/near-saturated high throughput curve for ZB-2p starting at $m/p = 2$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### 4. Consequential Decisions to Confirm\\n- **Memory Management:** For large $m$, activation memory increases linearly unless activation checkpointing is applied. We plan to fix activation checkpointing policy identically across all methods to ensure fair latency comparison.\\n- **Backward Splitting ($B$ vs $W$):** The backward pass must be decomposed into input gradient calculation ($B$) and weight gradient calculation ($W$), typically yielding execution time ratios $t_F : t_B : t_W \\\\approx 1 : 1 : 1$. We will record kernel timings to verify this balance.\", \"evidence\": \"- Environment inspection of /workspace revealed only `task.json`, with `track.md` stating: 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.'\\n- The environment has no GPU enabled and no preinstalled third-party Zero Bubble or Megatron-LM codebase.\\n- Theoretical foundation:\\n  * In standard 1F1B, the bubble rate is (p - 1) / (m + p - 1), which directly decreases as m increases.\\n  * In 1F1B-I with v=2, the bubble rate is (p - 1) / (v*m + p - 1), still dependent on 1/m.\\n  * In ZB-1p, splitting backward into B and W fills the steady-state bubble, but warmup/cooldown bubbles of size ~(p - 1)*F remain, yielding bubble fraction ~ (p - 1) / (3m + p - 1), which also improves with increasing m.\\n  * In ZB-2p, combining v=2 partitions with B/W splitting allows forward passes of partition 2 to overlap with partition 1 warmup and W to fill remaining bubbles, eliminating the bubble at m >= 2p and maintaining high throughput even at small m.\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Impact of communication overhead: 1F1B-I and ZB-2p double the number of cross-stage communications (v=2), which could introduce non-negligible communication overhead if network bandwidth is constrained.\", \"Activation memory pressure: ZB-2p holds activations across two virtual stages, which may limit the maximum achievable microbatch count m without out-of-memory errors compared to 1F1B.\"], \"assumptions\": [\"Computation time ratios follow standard Transformer scaling where forward (F), activation backward (B), and weight backward (W) approximate 1 : 1 : 1 (F \\u2248 B \\u2248 W).\", \"Per-microbatch batch size b and sequence length are held constant so that increasing m scales total global batch size without altering individual kernel latencies.\", \"GPU memory is sufficient (with standard selective activation checkpointing) to accommodate peak activation storage at the highest evaluated microbatch counts (m = 8p).\", \"P2P communication latency between pipeline stages is either non-blocking/overlapped or uniform across all stages.\"], \"stage\": \"plan\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "9GTUavneBdybpucP6_mM4As", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
