{"id": "816e7a59-b5f8-42d6-aa39-eee5726a38eb", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T23:20:18.729630", "source": "user", "parent_id": "bf488049-59c7-48d1-90f6-4913da1e8f44", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Your proposed experimental approach is sound and approved. Please proceed to develop the complete final experiment design document. Ensure it includes:\\n1. Thorough theoretical rationale and bubble rate formulations for each schedule (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p).\\n2. Rigorous experimental controls (e.g., fixed microbatch size, activation checkpointing, communication environment, hardware specs).\\n3. Detailed analysis plan (analytical bubble modeling, empirical throughput curves, metric definitions, and hypotheses testing criteria).\\n4. Feasibility assessment (hardware requirements, memory limits, and practical scheduling constraints).\\n\\nRemember that this is a design-only task, so do not execute runs or report fabricated empirical measurements.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "2873e022-30b3-482f-9712-f5f0946f4125", "timestamp": "2026-10-17T23:21:11.951730", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "816e7a59-b5f8-42d6-aa39-eee5726a38eb", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Complete Experimental Design: Investigating Throughput Scaling Across Pipeline Parallelism Schedules (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Theoretical Rationale and Bubble Rate Formulations\n\nPipeline parallelism (PP) partitions the layers of a deep neural network across $p$ sequential pipeline stages distributed over physical workers. In each training iteration, a global batch is split into $m$ microbatches. \n\n### 1.1 Computation Breakdown\nIn standard backpropagation, the backward pass computes two distinct gradients:\n1. **Activation gradients** ($\\nabla_x$, denoted as $B$): Needed immediately by preceding pipeline stages to continue backpropagation.\n2. **Weight/parameter gradients** ($\\nabla_w$, denoted as $W$): Needed only for the local optimizer update at the end of the iteration, lying strictly outside the critical backward propagation path.\n\nFor standard Transformer layers (e.g., self-attention and MLP blocks), computational complexity dictates that the backward pass requires approximately double the floating-point operations of the forward pass ($F$). When decomposed:\n$$t_F \\approx F, \\quad t_B \\approx B \\approx F, \\quad t_W \\approx W \\approx F \\implies t_{\\text{backward}} = t_B + t_W \\approx 2F$$\nTotal computation time per microbatch across all passes is:\n$$t_{\\text{comp}} = t_F + t_B + t_W \\approx 3F$$\n\n---\n\n### 1.2 Pipeline Schedule Mechanics and Bubble Rate Formulations\n\n#### 1. Standard 1F1B (One Forward, One Backward)\n- **Mechanism:** Stages execute a warmup of forward passes until stage $p-1$ begins, after which each worker strictly alternates one forward pass with one full backward pass ($B + W$ combined).\n- **Bubble Analysis:** Warmup takes $(p - 1)F$ and cooldown takes $(p - 1)(B + W) = 2(p - 1)F$.\n- **Total Bubble Time:**\n  $$T_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{1F1B}} = (p - 1)(F + B + W) = 3(p - 1)F$$\n- **Ideal Stage Computation Time:** $T_{\\text{work}} = 3mF$.\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\text{bubble}}$):**\n  $$r_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{1F1B}} = \\frac{T_{\\text{bubble}}}{T_{\\text{work}} + T_{\\text{bubble}}} = \\frac{3(p - 1)F}{3mF + 3(p - 1)F} = \\frac{p - 1}{m + p - 1}$$\n- **Behavior as $m$ Increases:** The bubble fraction decays as $\\mathcal{O}(p/m)$. For small microbatch counts (e.g., $m = p$), $r_{\\text{bubble}} \\approx 50\\%$. As $m \\to \\infty$, $r_{\\text{bubble}} \\to 0$. Hence, throughput improves substantially as $m$ increases.\n\n#### 2. Interleaved 1F1B (1F1B-I, with $v = 2$)\n- **Mechanism:** Each physical device hosts $v$ virtual stages (chunks). With $v = 2$, each worker hosts two distinct sub-models (chunk 0 and chunk 1). Stage execution times are scaled to $F/v$ and $2F/v$.\n- **Bubble Analysis:** By interleaving microbatches across virtual stages, the pipeline bubble duration is divided by $v$:\n  $$T_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{1F1B-I}} \\approx \\frac{3(p - 1)F}{v}$$\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\text{bubble}}$):**\n  $$r_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{1F1B-I}} \\approx \\frac{p - 1}{v \\cdot m + p - 1}$$\n- **Behavior as $m$ Increases:** While 1F1B-I cuts the idle bubble roughly in half compared to 1F1B, the bubble rate remains fundamentally proportional to $\\frac{p - 1}{2m + p - 1}$. At $m = p$, $r_{\\text{bubble}} \\approx 33\\%$. Throughput steadily improves as $m$ increases.\n\n#### 3. Zero Bubble 1-Partition (ZB-1p)\n- **Mechanism:** Each physical device holds 1 model partition. The backward pass is split into $B$ and $W$. $W$ computations are non-critical and scheduled during idle slots of the 1F1B steady state.\n- **Bubble Analysis:** In the 1F1B steady state, $W$ fills the gaps between $F$ and $B$. However, during the initial forward warmup (before the first backward activation gradient arrives at stage 0), no $W$ jobs are ready to run. Thus, a warmup bubble of $(p - 1)F$ remains unfillable.\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\text{bubble}}$):**\n  $$r_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{ZB-1p}} \\approx \\frac{(p - 1)F}{3mF + (p - 1)F} = \\frac{p - 1}{3m + p - 1}$$\n- **Behavior as $m$ Increases:** Because the unfillable warmup bubble is $(p - 1)F$ instead of $3(p - 1)F$, the bubble is reduced by $3\\times$ compared to 1F1B. However, it still depends on $m$: at $m = p$, $r_{\\text{bubble}} \\approx 25\\%$; at $m = 8p$, $r_{\\text{bubble}} \\approx 4\\%$. Throughput increases as $m$ grows.\n\n#### 4. Zero Bubble 2-Partition (ZB-2p, with $v = 2$)\n- **Mechanism:** Each worker hosts two virtual stages ($v = 2$), combined with decoupled $B$ and $W$ passes.\n- **Bubble Elimination:** Worker $i$ hosts stage $i$ (chunk 0) and stage $2p - 1 - i$ (chunk 1). While worker $i$ waits for chunk 0 backward dependencies, it executes forward passes ($F_1$) of chunk 1. The remaining pipeline gaps are filled by $W_0$ and $W_1$ jobs.\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\text{bubble}}$):**\n  - When $m \\ge 2p$, the schedule contains enough decoupled $F, B, W$ jobs across both virtual chunks to completely fill both warmup and cooldown idle phases:\n    $$r_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{ZB-2p}} \\approx 0 \\quad (\\text{for } m \\ge 2p)$$\n  - For $m \\in [p, 2p)$, $r_{\\text{bubble}}^{\\text{ZB-2p}} \\le \\frac{2p - m}{3m} \\approx 0\\% - 5\\%$.\n- **Behavior:** ZB-2p eliminates virtually all bubble time even at small microbatch counts ($m \\sim 2p$ or $m \\sim p$). Therefore, ZB-2p already reaches near-peak theoretical throughput with few microbatches and maintains flat, high throughput as $m$ increases, whereas 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p require significantly larger $m$ to approach that throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Rigorous Experimental Controls\n\n| Control Domain | Parameter | Specification & Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Hardware Environment** | Cluster Specification | 8 $\\times$ NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB (single HGX node, $p=8$) connected via 600 GB/s NVLink. |\n| | GPU Clocks & Power | Clocks locked to base/boost frequency (`nvidia-smi -pm 1 -ac 1215,1410`) to eliminate thermal throttling and frequency jitter. |\n| **Model Configuration** | Model Architecture | LLaMA-7B: 32 Transformer layers, hidden size $d = 4096$, 32 attention heads, sequence length $L = 2048$, vocab size 32,000. |\n| | Layer Partitioning | Homogeneous allocation: exactly 4 layers per stage for $p=8$ (for $v=2$, each chunk holds exactly 2 layers). |\n| | Computation Ratio | Verify $t_F : t_B : t_W \\approx 1 : 1 : 1$ via isolated single-stage microbenchmarking. |\n| **Batching & Execution** | Microbatch Size ($b$) | Fixed strictly at $b = 1$ sequence per microbatch. Isolates pipeline bubble effects by keeping kernel execution times identical across all sweeps. |\n| | Global Microbatch Sweeps | Microbatch count $m \\in \\{8, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64\\}$ corresponding to $m/p \\in \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8\\}$. |\n| **Memory & Overlap** | Activation Checkpointing | Uniform selective activation checkpointing applied across all four schedules to isolate schedule efficiency from recomputation overhead. |\n| | Communication Protocol | Non-blocking PyTorch P2P primitives (`torch.distributed.P2POp`) over dedicated CUDA communication streams with pre-allocated activation buffers. |\n| **Statistical Controls** | Warmup & Repetitions | 10 warmup iterations discarded; 30 steady-state iterations timed with CUDA events (`torch.cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)`). 5 independent runs per configuration. |\n| | Data Input | Synthetic randomly generated token tensors held in GPU memory to eliminate host-to-device I/O variability. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Analysis Plan\n\n### 3.1 Primary & Secondary Metrics\n1. **End-to-End Throughput ($\\mathcal{T}$):**\n   $$\\mathcal{T} = \\frac{m \\cdot b \\cdot L}{\\Delta t_{\\text{step}}} \\quad [\\text{tokens/second}]$$\n2. **Model TFLOPS per GPU:**\n   $$\\text{TFLOPS} = \\frac{96 \\times P \\times m \\cdot b \\cdot L}{\\Delta t_{\\text{step}} \\times p \\times 10^{12}}$$\n   where $P$ is active parameter count (7B).\n3. **Pipeline Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\text{bubble}}$):**\n   $$r_{\\text{bubble}} = 1 - \\frac{m \\cdot (t_F + t_B + t_W)}{p \\cdot \\Delta t_{\\text{step}}}$$\n   Cross-validated using Nsight Systems / PyTorch Profiler traces of GPU idle durations.\n4. **Peak Allocated GPU Memory:**\n   $$M_{\\text{peak}} = \\max_{i \\in [0, p-1]} \\text{max\\_memory\\_allocated}(i) \\quad [\\text{GB}]$$\n\n### 3.2 Analytical Bubble Modeling vs. Empirical Benchmarking\n- **Analytical Discrete-Event Simulation:** Model the schedule DAG with measured kernel durations ($t_F, t_B, t_W$) and P2P communication latencies ($t_{\\text{comm}}$). Compute theoretical schedule completion times $T_{\\text{sim}}(m, \\text{schedule})$.\n- **Empirical Curve Fitting:** Fit empirical throughput $\\mathcal{T}(m)$ to the theoretical saturation model:\n  $$\\mathcal{T}(m) = \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{max}} \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{C_{\\text{schedule}}}{m + C_{\\text{schedule}}}\\right)$$\n  where $C_{\\text{1F1B}} = p - 1$, $C_{\\text{1F1B-I}} = \\frac{p - 1}{2}$, $C_{\\text{ZB-1p}} = \\frac{p - 1}{3}$, and $C_{\\text{ZB-2p}} \\approx 0$ (for $m \\ge 2p$).\n\n### 3.3 Hypotheses Testing Criteria\nTo substantiate the hypothesis affirmatively, the empirical and analytical data must satisfy:\n1. **Positive Scaling for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p:**\n   $$\\frac{\\mathcal{T}(m=8p) - \\mathcal{T}(m=p)}{\\mathcal{T}(m=p)} > 0$$\n   - 1F1B throughput improves by $> 50\\%$ from $m=p$ to $m=8p$ ($p < 0.001$, one-sided Welch's t-test).\n   - 1F1B-I throughput improves by $> 30\\%$ ($p < 0.001$).\n   - ZB-1p throughput improves by $> 20\\%$ ($p < 0.001$).\n2. **High Throughput at Small $m$ for ZB-2p:**\n   - At $m = p$ and $m = 2p$, $\\mathcal{T}_{\\text{ZB-2p}} > \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{ZB-1p}} > \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{1F1B-I}} > \\mathcal{T}_{\\text{1F1B}}$ with statistical significance ($p < 0.01$).\n3. **Early Saturation of ZB-2p:**\n   - At $m = 2p$, ZB-2p achieves $\\ge 92\\%$ of its maximum observed throughput at $m = 8p$:\n     $$\\frac{\\mathcal{T}_{\\text{ZB-2p}}(m=2p)}{\\mathcal{T}_{\\text{ZB-2p}}(m=8p)} \\ge 0.92$$\n   - In contrast, at $m = 2p$, 1F1B reaches $< 70\\%$ and 1F1B-I reaches $< 80\\%$ of their respective peak throughputs.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Feasibility Assessment\n\n### 4.1 Memory Limits and Activation Footprint\n- **Peak Activation Stash:**\n  - In 1F1B, a worker stores activations for at most $p$ microbatches during steady state.\n  - In ZB-1p, delaying $W$ requires holding activations until $W$ executes; memory footprint is bounded at approximately $p + 1$ microbatches.\n  - In ZB-2p, activations must be maintained across 2 virtual stages. The maximum number of outstanding microbatch activations per device is bounded by $2p$.\n- **VRAM Budget Calculation for LLaMA-7B ($p=8, b=1, L=2048$):**\n  - Model parameters + FP16 gradients + FP32 optimizer states per stage: $\\sim 7.5\\text{ GB}$.\n  - Activation memory per microbatch per stage (with selective checkpointing): $\\sim 0.35\\text{ GB}$.\n  - At $m = 16$ ($2p$) in ZB-2p, maximum active activation footprint is $16 \\times 0.35\\text{ GB} \\approx 5.6\\text{ GB}$.\n  - Total memory consumption $\\approx 13.1\\text{ GB}$, comfortably below the 80 GB limit of NVIDIA A100/H100 GPUs. Even at $m = 64$ with un-checkpointed baselines, memory remains within feasible hardware thresholds.\n\n### 4.2 Practical Scheduling and autograd Constraints\n- **Decoupling $B$ and $W$:** PyTorch autograd combines $\\nabla_x$ and $\\nabla_w$ inside standard C++ engine routines. Splitting requires either:\n  1. Registering tensor hooks on layer weights to intercept intermediate activation gradients and queue weight gradient computations separately.\n  2. Utilizing Zero Bubble's custom autograd backward splitters (wrapping linear/attention projections into custom `torch.autograd.Function` instances that output $B$ and return a callable for $W$).\n- **Communication Overhead:**\n  - 1F1B-I and ZB-2p introduce $2\\times$ more P2P boundary exchanges because each worker processes two virtual stages.\n  - With intra-node NVLink bandwidth of 600 GB/s, transferring hidden activation tensors ($b \\times L \\times d \\times 2\\text{ bytes} = 1 \\times 2048 \\times 4096 \\times 2 \\approx 16.8\\text{ MB}$) takes $< 0.03\\text{ ms}$, which is negligible compared to layer compute times ($t_F \\approx 15-25\\text{ ms}$). Thus, communication is fully masked by computation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary Matrix of Expected Results\n\n| Schedule | Virtual Stages ($v$) | Backward Split ($B / W$) | Bubble Rate ($m = p$) | Bubble Rate ($m = 2p$) | Bubble Rate ($m = 8p$) | Throughput Sensitivity to $m$ |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **1F1B** | 1 | No (Combined) | $\\sim 50.0\\%$ | $\\sim 33.3\\%$ | $\\sim 11.1\\%$ | **High:** Steep scaling as $m$ increases |\n| **1F1B-I** | 2 | No (Combined) | $\\sim 33.3\\%$ | $\\sim 20.0\\%$ | $\\sim 5.9\\%$ | **Moderate-High:** Steady improvement with $m$ |\n| **ZB-1p** | 1 | Yes (Decoupled) | $\\sim 25.0\\%$ | $\\sim 14.3\\%$ | $\\sim 4.0\\%$ | **Moderate:** Noticeable improvement as warmup amortizes |\n| **ZB-2p** | 2 | Yes (Decoupled) | $\\sim 3.0\\%$ | $\\approx \\mathbf{0.0\\%}$ | $\\approx \\mathbf{0.0\\%}$ | **Flat / Invariant:** Sustains near-peak throughput at low $m$ |", "evidence": "1. Workspace Inspection: The repository at /workspace is a design-only workspace containing only task.json and track.md ('Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'). No compute was run and no empirical results were fabricated.\n2. Schedule Mathematics & Formulations:\n   - 1F1B bubble rate = (p - 1) / (m + p - 1)\n   - 1F1B-I (v=2) bubble rate = (p - 1) / (2m + p - 1)\n   - ZB-1p bubble rate = (p - 1) / (3m + p - 1)\n   - ZB-2p bubble rate ≈ 0 for m >= 2p due to virtual stage overlapping and B/W decoupling.\n3. Control Feasibility: Activation memory for LLaMA-7B at b=1, L=2048 is ~0.35 GB per microbatch per stage, ensuring that ZB-2p requiring 2p microbatch stashes (~5.6 GB activation memory) easily operates within an 80 GB VRAM envelope.", "assumptions": ["Per-microbatch execution times for forward (F), activation backward (B), and weight backward (W) are roughly balanced (t_F ≈ t_B ≈ t_W), consistent with Transformer matrix multiplication FLOP profiles.", "Microbatch size b=1 and sequence length L=2048 are held constant across sweeps to isolate pipeline schedule bubble overhead from arithmetic intensity shifts.", "Hardware interconnect (NVLink >= 600 GB/s) has sufficient bandwidth such that P2P tensor transfers are completely masked behind layer compute times.", "GPU device memory is sufficient to store outstanding activations for up to 2p microbatches in ZB-2p and 1F1B baselines under selective activation checkpointing."], "uncertainties": ["Slight deviations in kernel runtime ratios (e.g., if t_B != t_W due to non-GEMM layer normalization or attention softmax overhead) could leave minor micro-bubbles in ZB-2p.", "Network jitter or PCIe bottlenecks on multi-node clusters (without NVLink) could introduce P2P communication stalls in 2-partition schedules."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_505513__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_505513__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"evidence\": \"1. Workspace Inspection: The repository at /workspace is a design-only workspace containing only task.json and track.md ('Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'). No compute was run and no empirical results were fabricated.\\n2. Schedule Mathematics & Formulations:\\n   - 1F1B bubble rate = (p - 1) / (m + p - 1)\\n   - 1F1B-I (v=2) bubble rate = (p - 1) / (2m + p - 1)\\n   - ZB-1p bubble rate = (p - 1) / (3m + p - 1)\\n   - ZB-2p bubble rate \\u2248 0 for m >= 2p due to virtual stage overlapping and B/W decoupling.\\n3. Control Feasibility: Activation memory for LLaMA-7B at b=1, L=2048 is ~0.35 GB per microbatch per stage, ensuring that ZB-2p requiring 2p microbatch stashes (~5.6 GB activation memory) easily operates within an 80 GB VRAM envelope.\", \"answer\": \"# Complete Experimental Design: Investigating Throughput Scaling Across Pipeline Parallelism Schedules (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Theoretical Rationale and Bubble Rate Formulations\\n\\nPipeline parallelism (PP) partitions the layers of a deep neural network across $p$ sequential pipeline stages distributed over physical workers. In each training iteration, a global batch is split into $m$ microbatches. \\n\\n### 1.1 Computation Breakdown\\nIn standard backpropagation, the backward pass computes two distinct gradients:\\n1. **Activation gradients** ($\\\\nabla_x$, denoted as $B$): Needed immediately by preceding pipeline stages to continue backpropagation.\\n2. **Weight/parameter gradients** ($\\\\nabla_w$, denoted as $W$): Needed only for the local optimizer update at the end of the iteration, lying strictly outside the critical backward propagation path.\\n\\nFor standard Transformer layers (e.g., self-attention and MLP blocks), computational complexity dictates that the backward pass requires approximately double the floating-point operations of the forward pass ($F$). When decomposed:\\n$$t_F \\\\approx F, \\\\quad t_B \\\\approx B \\\\approx F, \\\\quad t_W \\\\approx W \\\\approx F \\\\implies t_{\\\\text{backward}} = t_B + t_W \\\\approx 2F$$\\nTotal computation time per microbatch across all passes is:\\n$$t_{\\\\text{comp}} = t_F + t_B + t_W \\\\approx 3F$$\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1.2 Pipeline Schedule Mechanics and Bubble Rate Formulations\\n\\n#### 1. Standard 1F1B (One Forward, One Backward)\\n- **Mechanism:** Stages execute a warmup of forward passes until stage $p-1$ begins, after which each worker strictly alternates one forward pass with one full backward pass ($B + W$ combined).\\n- **Bubble Analysis:** Warmup takes $(p - 1)F$ and cooldown takes $(p - 1)(B + W) = 2(p - 1)F$.\\n- **Total Bubble Time:**\\n  $$T_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{1F1B}} = (p - 1)(F + B + W) = 3(p - 1)F$$\\n- **Ideal Stage Computation Time:** $T_{\\\\text{work}} = 3mF$.\\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\\\text{bubble}}$):**\\n  $$r_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{1F1B}} = \\\\frac{T_{\\\\text{bubble}}}{T_{\\\\text{work}} + T_{\\\\text{bubble}}} = \\\\frac{3(p - 1)F}{3mF + 3(p - 1)F} = \\\\frac{p - 1}{m + p - 1}$$\\n- **Behavior as $m$ Increases:** The bubble fraction decays as $\\\\mathcal{O}(p/m)$. For small microbatch counts (e.g., $m = p$), $r_{\\\\text{bubble}} \\\\approx 50\\\\%$. As $m \\\\to \\\\infty$, $r_{\\\\text{bubble}} \\\\to 0$. Hence, throughput improves substantially as $m$ increases.\\n\\n#### 2. Interleaved 1F1B (1F1B-I, with $v = 2$)\\n- **Mechanism:** Each physical device hosts $v$ virtual stages (chunks). With $v = 2$, each worker hosts two distinct sub-models (chunk 0 and chunk 1). Stage execution times are scaled to $F/v$ and $2F/v$.\\n- **Bubble Analysis:** By interleaving microbatches across virtual stages, the pipeline bubble duration is divided by $v$:\\n  $$T_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{1F1B-I}} \\\\approx \\\\frac{3(p - 1)F}{v}$$\\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\\\text{bubble}}$):**\\n  $$r_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{1F1B-I}} \\\\approx \\\\frac{p - 1}{v \\\\cdot m + p - 1}$$\\n- **Behavior as $m$ Increases:** While 1F1B-I cuts the idle bubble roughly in half compared to 1F1B, the bubble rate remains fundamentally proportional to $\\\\frac{p - 1}{2m + p - 1}$. At $m = p$, $r_{\\\\text{bubble}} \\\\approx 33\\\\%$. Throughput steadily improves as $m$ increases.\\n\\n#### 3. Zero Bubble 1-Partition (ZB-1p)\\n- **Mechanism:** Each physical device holds 1 model partition. The backward pass is split into $B$ and $W$. $W$ computations are non-critical and scheduled during idle slots of the 1F1B steady state.\\n- **Bubble Analysis:** In the 1F1B steady state, $W$ fills the gaps between $F$ and $B$. However, during the initial forward warmup (before the first backward activation gradient arrives at stage 0), no $W$ jobs are ready to run. Thus, a warmup bubble of $(p - 1)F$ remains unfillable.\\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\\\text{bubble}}$):**\\n  $$r_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{ZB-1p}} \\\\approx \\\\frac{(p - 1)F}{3mF + (p - 1)F} = \\\\frac{p - 1}{3m + p - 1}$$\\n- **Behavior as $m$ Increases:** Because the unfillable warmup bubble is $(p - 1)F$ instead of $3(p - 1)F$, the bubble is reduced by $3\\\\times$ compared to 1F1B. However, it still depends on $m$: at $m = p$, $r_{\\\\text{bubble}} \\\\approx 25\\\\%$; at $m = 8p$, $r_{\\\\text{bubble}} \\\\approx 4\\\\%$. Throughput increases as $m$ grows.\\n\\n#### 4. Zero Bubble 2-Partition (ZB-2p, with $v = 2$)\\n- **Mechanism:** Each worker hosts two virtual stages ($v = 2$), combined with decoupled $B$ and $W$ passes.\\n- **Bubble Elimination:** Worker $i$ hosts stage $i$ (chunk 0) and stage $2p - 1 - i$ (chunk 1). While worker $i$ waits for chunk 0 backward dependencies, it executes forward passes ($F_1$) of chunk 1. The remaining pipeline gaps are filled by $W_0$ and $W_1$ jobs.\\n- **Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\\\text{bubble}}$):**\\n  - When $m \\\\ge 2p$, the schedule contains enough decoupled $F, B, W$ jobs across both virtual chunks to completely fill both warmup and cooldown idle phases:\\n    $$r_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{ZB-2p}} \\\\approx 0 \\\\quad (\\\\text{for } m \\\\ge 2p)$$\\n  - For $m \\\\in [p, 2p)$, $r_{\\\\text{bubble}}^{\\\\text{ZB-2p}} \\\\le \\\\frac{2p - m}{3m} \\\\approx 0\\\\% - 5\\\\%$.\\n- **Behavior:** ZB-2p eliminates virtually all bubble time even at small microbatch counts ($m \\\\sim 2p$ or $m \\\\sim p$). Therefore, ZB-2p already reaches near-peak theoretical throughput with few microbatches and maintains flat, high throughput as $m$ increases, whereas 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p require significantly larger $m$ to approach that throughput.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Rigorous Experimental Controls\\n\\n| Control Domain | Parameter | Specification & Rationale |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Hardware Environment** | Cluster Specification | 8 $\\\\times$ NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB (single HGX node, $p=8$) connected via 600 GB/s NVLink. |\\n| | GPU Clocks & Power | Clocks locked to base/boost frequency (`nvidia-smi -pm 1 -ac 1215,1410`) to eliminate thermal throttling and frequency jitter. |\\n| **Model Configuration** | Model Architecture | LLaMA-7B: 32 Transformer layers, hidden size $d = 4096$, 32 attention heads, sequence length $L = 2048$, vocab size 32,000. |\\n| | Layer Partitioning | Homogeneous allocation: exactly 4 layers per stage for $p=8$ (for $v=2$, each chunk holds exactly 2 layers). |\\n| | Computation Ratio | Verify $t_F : t_B : t_W \\\\approx 1 : 1 : 1$ via isolated single-stage microbenchmarking. |\\n| **Batching & Execution** | Microbatch Size ($b$) | Fixed strictly at $b = 1$ sequence per microbatch. Isolates pipeline bubble effects by keeping kernel execution times identical across all sweeps. |\\n| | Global Microbatch Sweeps | Microbatch count $m \\\\in \\\\{8, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64\\\\}$ corresponding to $m/p \\\\in \\\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8\\\\}$. |\\n| **Memory & Overlap** | Activation Checkpointing | Uniform selective activation checkpointing applied across all four schedules to isolate schedule efficiency from recomputation overhead. |\\n| | Communication Protocol | Non-blocking PyTorch P2P primitives (`torch.distributed.P2POp`) over dedicated CUDA communication streams with pre-allocated activation buffers. |\\n| **Statistical Controls** | Warmup & Repetitions | 10 warmup iterations discarded; 30 steady-state iterations timed with CUDA events (`torch.cuda.Event(enable_timing=True)`). 5 independent runs per configuration. |\\n| | Data Input | Synthetic randomly generated token tensors held in GPU memory to eliminate host-to-device I/O variability. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Detailed Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 3.1 Primary & Secondary Metrics\\n1. **End-to-End Throughput ($\\\\mathcal{T}$):**\\n   $$\\\\mathcal{T} = \\\\frac{m \\\\cdot b \\\\cdot L}{\\\\Delta t_{\\\\text{step}}} \\\\quad [\\\\text{tokens/second}]$$\\n2. **Model TFLOPS per GPU:**\\n   $$\\\\text{TFLOPS} = \\\\frac{96 \\\\times P \\\\times m \\\\cdot b \\\\cdot L}{\\\\Delta t_{\\\\text{step}} \\\\times p \\\\times 10^{12}}$$\\n   where $P$ is active parameter count (7B).\\n3. **Pipeline Bubble Fraction ($r_{\\\\text{bubble}}$):**\\n   $$r_{\\\\text{bubble}} = 1 - \\\\frac{m \\\\cdot (t_F + t_B + t_W)}{p \\\\cdot \\\\Delta t_{\\\\text{step}}}$$\\n   Cross-validated using Nsight Systems / PyTorch Profiler traces of GPU idle durations.\\n4. **Peak Allocated GPU Memory:**\\n   $$M_{\\\\text{peak}} = \\\\max_{i \\\\in [0, p-1]} \\\\text{max\\\\_memory\\\\_allocated}(i) \\\\quad [\\\\text{GB}]$$\\n\\n### 3.2 Analytical Bubble Modeling vs. Empirical Benchmarking\\n- **Analytical Discrete-Event Simulation:** Model the schedule DAG with measured kernel durations ($t_F, t_B, t_W$) and P2P communication latencies ($t_{\\\\text{comm}}$). Compute theoretical schedule completion times $T_{\\\\text{sim}}(m, \\\\text{schedule})$.\\n- **Empirical Curve Fitting:** Fit empirical throughput $\\\\mathcal{T}(m)$ to the theoretical saturation model:\\n  $$\\\\mathcal{T}(m) = \\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{max}} \\\\cdot \\\\left(1 - \\\\frac{C_{\\\\text{schedule}}}{m + C_{\\\\text{schedule}}}\\\\right)$$\\n  where $C_{\\\\text{1F1B}} = p - 1$, $C_{\\\\text{1F1B-I}} = \\\\frac{p - 1}{2}$, $C_{\\\\text{ZB-1p}} = \\\\frac{p - 1}{3}$, and $C_{\\\\text{ZB-2p}} \\\\approx 0$ (for $m \\\\ge 2p$).\\n\\n### 3.3 Hypotheses Testing Criteria\\nTo substantiate the hypothesis affirmatively, the empirical and analytical data must satisfy:\\n1. **Positive Scaling for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p:**\\n   $$\\\\frac{\\\\mathcal{T}(m=8p) - \\\\mathcal{T}(m=p)}{\\\\mathcal{T}(m=p)} > 0$$\\n   - 1F1B throughput improves by $> 50\\\\%$ from $m=p$ to $m=8p$ ($p < 0.001$, one-sided Welch's t-test).\\n   - 1F1B-I throughput improves by $> 30\\\\%$ ($p < 0.001$).\\n   - ZB-1p throughput improves by $> 20\\\\%$ ($p < 0.001$).\\n2. **High Throughput at Small $m$ for ZB-2p:**\\n   - At $m = p$ and $m = 2p$, $\\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{ZB-2p}} > \\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{ZB-1p}} > \\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{1F1B-I}} > \\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{1F1B}}$ with statistical significance ($p < 0.01$).\\n3. **Early Saturation of ZB-2p:**\\n   - At $m = 2p$, ZB-2p achieves $\\\\ge 92\\\\%$ of its maximum observed throughput at $m = 8p$:\\n     $$\\\\frac{\\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{ZB-2p}}(m=2p)}{\\\\mathcal{T}_{\\\\text{ZB-2p}}(m=8p)} \\\\ge 0.92$$\\n   - In contrast, at $m = 2p$, 1F1B reaches $< 70\\\\%$ and 1F1B-I reaches $< 80\\\\%$ of their respective peak throughputs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Feasibility Assessment\\n\\n### 4.1 Memory Limits and Activation Footprint\\n- **Peak Activation Stash:**\\n  - In 1F1B, a worker stores activations for at most $p$ microbatches during steady state.\\n  - In ZB-1p, delaying $W$ requires holding activations until $W$ executes; memory footprint is bounded at approximately $p + 1$ microbatches.\\n  - In ZB-2p, activations must be maintained across 2 virtual stages. The maximum number of outstanding microbatch activations per device is bounded by $2p$.\\n- **VRAM Budget Calculation for LLaMA-7B ($p=8, b=1, L=2048$):**\\n  - Model parameters + FP16 gradients + FP32 optimizer states per stage: $\\\\sim 7.5\\\\text{ GB}$.\\n  - Activation memory per microbatch per stage (with selective checkpointing): $\\\\sim 0.35\\\\text{ GB}$.\\n  - At $m = 16$ ($2p$) in ZB-2p, maximum active activation footprint is $16 \\\\times 0.35\\\\text{ GB} \\\\approx 5.6\\\\text{ GB}$.\\n  - Total memory consumption $\\\\approx 13.1\\\\text{ GB}$, comfortably below the 80 GB limit of NVIDIA A100/H100 GPUs. Even at $m = 64$ with un-checkpointed baselines, memory remains within feasible hardware thresholds.\\n\\n### 4.2 Practical Scheduling and autograd Constraints\\n- **Decoupling $B$ and $W$:** PyTorch autograd combines $\\\\nabla_x$ and $\\\\nabla_w$ inside standard C++ engine routines. Splitting requires either:\\n  1. Registering tensor hooks on layer weights to intercept intermediate activation gradients and queue weight gradient computations separately.\\n  2. Utilizing Zero Bubble's custom autograd backward splitters (wrapping linear/attention projections into custom `torch.autograd.Function` instances that output $B$ and return a callable for $W$).\\n- **Communication Overhead:**\\n  - 1F1B-I and ZB-2p introduce $2\\\\times$ more P2P boundary exchanges because each worker processes two virtual stages.\\n  - With intra-node NVLink bandwidth of 600 GB/s, transferring hidden activation tensors ($b \\\\times L \\\\times d \\\\times 2\\\\text{ bytes} = 1 \\\\times 2048 \\\\times 4096 \\\\times 2 \\\\approx 16.8\\\\text{ MB}$) takes $< 0.03\\\\text{ ms}$, which is negligible compared to layer compute times ($t_F \\\\approx 15-25\\\\text{ ms}$). Thus, communication is fully masked by computation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Summary Matrix of Expected Results\\n\\n| Schedule | Virtual Stages ($v$) | Backward Split ($B / W$) | Bubble Rate ($m = p$) | Bubble Rate ($m = 2p$) | Bubble Rate ($m = 8p$) | Throughput Sensitivity to $m$ |\\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\\n| **1F1B** | 1 | No (Combined) | $\\\\sim 50.0\\\\%$ | $\\\\sim 33.3\\\\%$ | $\\\\sim 11.1\\\\%$ | **High:** Steep scaling as $m$ increases |\\n| **1F1B-I** | 2 | No (Combined) | $\\\\sim 33.3\\\\%$ | $\\\\sim 20.0\\\\%$ | $\\\\sim 5.9\\\\%$ | **Moderate-High:** Steady improvement with $m$ |\\n| **ZB-1p** | 1 | Yes (Decoupled) | $\\\\sim 25.0\\\\%$ | $\\\\sim 14.3\\\\%$ | $\\\\sim 4.0\\\\%$ | **Moderate:** Noticeable improvement as warmup amortizes |\\n| **ZB-2p** | 2 | Yes (Decoupled) | $\\\\sim 3.0\\\\%$ | $\\\\approx \\\\mathbf{0.0\\\\%}$ | $\\\\approx \\\\mathbf{0.0\\\\%}$ | **Flat / Invariant:** Sustains near-peak throughput at low $m$ |\", \"assumptions\": [\"Per-microbatch execution times for forward (F), activation backward (B), and weight backward (W) are roughly balanced (t_F \\u2248 t_B \\u2248 t_W), consistent with Transformer matrix multiplication FLOP profiles.\", \"Microbatch size b=1 and sequence length L=2048 are held constant across sweeps to isolate pipeline schedule bubble overhead from arithmetic intensity shifts.\", \"Hardware interconnect (NVLink >= 600 GB/s) has sufficient bandwidth such that P2P tensor transfers are completely masked behind layer compute times.\", \"GPU device memory is sufficient to store outstanding activations for up to 2p microbatches in ZB-2p and 1F1B baselines under selective activation checkpointing.\"], \"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Completed full experiment design for throughput scaling across 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, and ZB-2p.\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Slight deviations in kernel runtime ratios (e.g., if t_B != t_W due to non-GEMM layer normalization or attention softmax overhead) could leave minor micro-bubbles in ZB-2p.\", \"Network jitter or PCIe bottlenecks on multi-node clusters (without NVLink) could introduce P2P communication stalls in 2-partition schedules.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "I2XUaopp_5zj9Q_q07HwBA", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
